  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 351 AM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nToday...Latest water vapor imagery shows the apex of a trough \ndraped across the Great Lakes region and continuing eastward. \nMeanwhile to our west, ridging is in place over the \nPacNorthwest, thus leaving us locally saddled between these \nfeatures and beneath a 50-70kt jetstreak. Surface high pressure \nwill hold firm over the northern plains today although \nconditions will not be as breezy and sunny as they were \nyesterday. Weak remnant upper-forcing from the departing trough \nwill allow for scattered mid to high level cloud cover. With the\nreturn of NW flow, highs this afternoon are expected to warm \nonly into the low to mid 60s. Although, a few sites across \nsouthwestern MN could see closer to 70 degrees. Lows tonight \nwill range in the 40s. \n\nFriday and this weekend...As mentioned in the previous discussion, \nFriday is a transition day between a cooler and a much warmer \nairmass that arrives this weekend. During the pre-dawn hours of \nFriday, a weak disturbance develops over the central plains and \nmoves northeast. As this occurs, a weak LLJ 30-40kts will increase \nover Iowa and have enough lift to generate a low-end potential\nfor a short period of showers Friday morning near the MN/IA \nborder. SW'ly flow becomes dominate and will allow for warm \nadvection to return with highs in to the 70s. By Saturday, \nrobust low pressure sweeps across the Canadian Prairies which \nforces warm advection to kick into high gear. Winds will become \ngusty and becoming as strong as 30-40 mph. Forecast highs will \nreach the low to mid-80s. Given the drier airmass in place \nprecip will not be of concern here. It is more so the gusty \nwinds combined with very warm temperatures which maintain a \nconcern for near- critical fire weather conditions. By Sunday, \nthe low pressure system tracks farther east however lingering \nwarmth and breezy conditions could make elevated fire weather \nconditions through Sunday afternoon. \n\nBy Sunday night into early next week, a cold front dives\nsoutheast into MN/WI. Much cooler air follows in tow behind \nthis cold front as temperatures will only reach the 50s/lower \n60s and lows in the upper 30s/lower 40s for the first half of \nthe week. As for precip associated with this front, PoPs have \ndecreased slightly with the latest NBM simply due to \nuncertainties with the slow progression of this wave and \nremnants of Hurricane Isaias.",
